  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nContinued northwest flow with 850mb temperatures holding around -5C \ntoday will allow scattered lake effect rain and snow showers to \npersist this morning. The showers gradually taper off and decrease \nin coverage this afternoon as it starts to slowly warm aloft. \nSurface temperatures will remain at or above freezing for most \nlocations early this morning so snow amounts were lowered with this \nupdate. Most locations won't see any accumulation. A coating to \nperhaps 1 inch of snow is possible for the higher hilltops of \nCentral NY; mainly in Cortland, Madison and northern Chenango \ncounties. Temperatures gradually rise into the upper 30s to mid-40s \nthis afternoon. Tonight's low temperature forecast was increased, \ninto the low to mid-30s for most locations. The lake effect cloud \ncover will remain widespread through the night, helping to keep the \ntemperatures more elevated.\n\nThe 850mb ridge axis does not pass through our area until Monday \nnight. Therefore, we remain locked in the cool, cloudy northwest \nflow pattern on Monday. Wouldn't be surprised to see pockets of \ndrizzle lingering into Monday under the thick stratus cloud deck. \nTemperatures remain cool only in the 40's on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFor Tuesday through Sunday: General upper level ridging remains over \nthe eastern US. There could be a very weak disturbance or front \npassing through Thursday night or Friday. Otherwise, high pressure \nlooks to remain in control right through Saturday (if current timing \nholds). By Sunday, a more well defined cold front will begin to \napproach the area from the west. Some guidance keeps it slower and \nback to the west, leading to one more very mild day in the upper 50s \nto low 60s. We will have to keep an eye on the timing of this \nfrontal feature.\n\nTemperatures will see a gradual warming trend through the \nweek, reaching about 15 degrees above average for daytime highs by \nFriday through Sunday (50's and low 60's). With the generally clear \nskies and light winds, overnight lows will be on the seasonably cool \nside, mainly in the 30's through the week. Overall a very pleasant \nand fair stretch of late fall weather looks to be in store.",
